WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.519
In this lesson we're looking at per field confidence scores. When a model

2
00:00:04.519 --> 00:00:09.560
extracts structured data it can emit a separate score for every field it fills

3
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.840
in. We'll cover why that matters, how to elicit those scores through schema

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:20.479
design, and how routing policy uses them to send only uncertain fields back to

5
00:00:20.479 --> 00:00:25.639
the human reviewer. Let's get into it. A record comes back from extraction

6
00:00:25.639 --> 00:00:29.680
carrying one confidence number for the whole thing and that number is doing

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.759
more hiding than reporting. Field level confidence is the alternative, a score

8
00:00:34.759 --> 00:00:39.599
the model emits for each individual extracted value rather than one number

9
00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:45.080
for the whole record. That granularity is what makes targeted routing possible. The

10
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.840
routing threshold is the cutoff those scores get compared against. Fields at or

11
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.240
above it are auto accepted and flow downstream and fields below it get queued

12
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.680
for a human reviewer. Calibration is the check that decides whether any of those

13
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.919
numbers deserve your trust by measuring reported confidence against real

14
00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:09.120
accuracy on labeled data. The problem with a single record level

15
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.080
confidence score is that it hides where the model is actually struggling. Imagine

16
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.239
a record that scores 0.85 overall. That sounds solid but what if the date field

17
00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:25.599
inside that record is sitting at 0.4? The aggregate number masked the weak

18
00:01:25.599 --> 00:01:30.720
spot entirely. A human reviewer who gets the whole record still has to wade

19
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.080
through every high confidence field to find the one that actually needed

20
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.879
attention. That wasted effort scales badly in a high volume extraction

21
00:01:39.879 --> 00:01:45.080
pipeline. Per field scores fix this by surfacing exactly where the uncertainty

22
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.080
lives so downstream routing can act precisely on the problem field rather

23
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.040
than re-inspecting the entire record. The contrast between record level and field

24
00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.519
level scoring decides how much work reaches your reviewers. With a record

25
00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:05.800
level score one number covers the full extraction and per record routing sends

26
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.160
the entire record to human review whenever any single field falls below

27
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.960
the threshold. That hands reviewers a lot of work they didn't need to do. Field

28
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.679
level scoring changes the routing unit from the record to the individual value.

29
00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:25.240
Each field carries its own score so per field routing acts on each value

30
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:31.559
independently. A customer name at 0.95 passes straight through. An invoice date

31
00:02:31.559 --> 00:02:37.720
at 0.38 goes to a reviewer. Per field routing is more efficient but it needs

32
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.559
downstream systems that can accept partially reviewed records. Per record

33
00:02:42.559 --> 00:02:46.800
routing is simpler and easier to audit which is why some compliance heavy

34
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.639
environments still prefer it even at the cost of higher reviewer workload. Here's

35
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:56.600
the thing to understand before you build any of this. There is no native

36
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.679
confidence field inside the model. CLAW doesn't hand you a calibrated

37
00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:05.919
probability from its internal state. What you get is a verbalized self-report. A

38
00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:10.440
number the model generates because you asked it to. So getting per field

39
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:14.919
confidence comes down to prompt and schema design. You define a structured

40
00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:19.360
output where each field has a value key and a confidence key where the model

41
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.320
writes its own estimate on a 0 to 1 scale. A self-reported score is just the

42
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.080
model's opinion of itself and that opinion can be badly wrong. The number

43
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.919
means nothing until you check it against a labeled held out set. That calibration

44
00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:39.479
is what turns a self-report into something you can route on. Choose which

45
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.119
high-stakes fields get a confident sibling based on downstream risk. Once

46
00:03:45.119 --> 00:03:48.880
the model returns scores for each field your routing logic reads them against a

47
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.600
threshold. Fields that score at or above the cutoff are auto accepted and move

48
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.919
downstream without any human involvement. Fields that fall below the cutoff are

49
00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:04.440
flagged and queued for a reviewer. In some systems there is also a borderline

50
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.800
zone near the threshold where a secondary check applies or where a

51
00:04:08.839 --> 00:04:14.960
tighter threshold kicks in for specific field types. The exact policy depends on

52
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.079
the business stakes and the reviewer capacity available but the mechanism is

53
00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:24.760
the same. You compare the score to a threshold and route accordingly. In

54
00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.000
production you will almost never see a record where every field is high

55
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.679
confidence or every field is low confidence. The typical case is mixed. A

56
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.720
record might have a customer name at 0.95, a total amount at 0.88, and an

57
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.239
invoice date at 0.38. The name and amount flow straight through. The date goes to

58
00:04:47.239 --> 00:04:51.399
the review queue. This selective escalation is the real value of per

59
00:04:51.399 --> 00:04:56.320
field scoring. Reviewers handle only the uncertain portion of the work rather

60
00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.200
than re-examining entire records that are mostly correct. That keeps throughput

61
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:06.200
high while still catching the fields that actually need attention. There is a

62
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.040
failure mode that trips up a lot of teams when they first deploy per field

63
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:15.040
confidence scoring. They take the model's confidence scores at face value and set

64
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.440
thresholds on those numbers without ever checking whether the scores are accurate.

65
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.279
A model might confidently report 0.9 on a field type where it is actually only

66
00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:31.040
correct 60% of the time. If routing trusts that 0.9 and auto accepts the

67
00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.200
field, errors flow straight into production. Calibration is how you catch

68
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.320
that mismatch. It measures reported scores against real accuracy on labeled

69
00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:46.320
data and it is what makes those numbers safe to route on before they drive real

70
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:52.440
decisions. A score for the whole record and a score for each field are not the

71
00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.959
same instrument. The record score says things look broadly okay while a field

72
00:05:58.079 --> 00:06:03.279
score says the customer name is fine but the invoice date is unsure. That

73
00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:08.480
difference decides whether a reviewer gets a whole record or one value. Just

74
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.799
remember a self-reported number is only trustworthy once you've checked it

75
00:06:12.799 --> 00:06:19.920
against labeled data. That brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next lesson.

